 # ItsDangerous
 
-... so better sign this
+Sign data with a secret key so you can hand it to an untrusted place (a cookie,
+a URL, an emailed link) and know it was not changed when it comes back.
 
-Various helpers to pass data to untrusted environments and to get it
-back safe and sound. Data is cryptographically signed to ensure that a
-token has not been tampered with.
+[![PyPI](https://img.shields.io/pypi/v/itsdangerous)](https://pypi.org/project/itsdangerous/)
+[![Python](https://img.shields.io/pypi/pyversions/itsdangerous)](https://pypi.org/project/itsdangerous/)
+[![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-BSD--3--Clause-blue)](LICENSE.txt)
+[![Tests](https://github.com/pallets/itsdangerous/actions/workflows/tests.yaml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/pallets/itsdangerous/actions/workflows/tests.yaml)
 
-It's possible to customize how data is serialized. Data is compressed as
-needed. A timestamp can be added and verified automatically while
-loading a token.
+```bash
+pip install itsdangerous   # Python 3.10+
+```
 
+```python
+from itsdangerous import URLSafeSerializer, BadSignature
 
-## A Simple Example
+s = URLSafeSerializer("secret key", salt="auth")
+token = s.dumps({"id": 5, "name": "itsdangerous"})
+s.loads(token)          # {'id': 5, 'name': 'itsdangerous'}
+s.loads(token + "x")    # raises BadSignature: the token was changed
+```
 
-Here's how you could generate a token for transmitting a user's id and
-name between web requests.
+**Signed, not encrypted:** anyone can read the payload; nobody without the key can
+change it. Need expiry? Use `URLSafeTimedSerializer` and `loads(token, max_age=3600)`.
 
